window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-7816828f15ef5632"]={"key":"ny_main-7816828f15ef5632","group":"ny_main","uid":"7816828f15ef5632","rank":13,"title":"crownridge executive communications roadmap","occupation":"producers_directors","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are the producer-director at Crownridge Housing Trust. Rafael Castellanos needs a customer communications roadmap by Friday so he can decide whether Zeynep Quintero is ready to schedule an executive briefing and staged resident updates about the major repairs programme. Sigrid Zalewski and Ivo Laurent are contributing to the material, Paloma Bhandari is part of the stakeholder group, and Flintridge Freight handles the printed resident packs. One contributor has warned that their approval may not arrive in time for the normal editorial-review procedure, and people have already started treating draft publication dates as approved commitments. Produce one executive-ready communications project roadmap that lets Rafael make the scheduling decision without having to ask you to interpret it. It needs to show the planned sequence, decision points, ownership, assumptions, dependencies, and what happens if the normal review path cannot be completed. Cover both the executive briefing and the staged resident updates, including the point at which material can move from editorial work into operational release. Make the document clear about what is still provisional, and actively prevent readers or delivery partners from treating planning dates as confirmed releases. Do not invent programme facts, resident impacts, approval outcomes, or delivery commitments that have not been established; identify assumptions instead. Keep the roadmap to no more than three pages, make it suitable for a senior decision maker who needs to scan it quickly, and write it so the working team can use it without asking the author to explain the status logic. Do not turn it into a system manual or a transcript of correspondence. Write the file as `crownridge_executive_communications_roadmap.docx` in the top level of the working directory you start in, using exactly that filename.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `crownridge_executive_communications_roadmap.docx` — An executive-ready project roadmap for the executive briefing and staged resident communications concerning the major repairs programme. (Must be no more than three pages when rendered.; Must distinguish editorial readiness, stakeholder approval, operational release, and post-release follow-up.; Must identify owners, decision points, assumptions, dependencies, exception handling, and status of dates.; Must not present provisional dates as confirmed releases or invent unsupported programme facts.; Must be usable by a senior decision maker and the working communications team without oral explanation.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/crownridge_executive_communications_roadmap.docx","bytes":46545,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/83bb524f000ddd1a74574726ffab582b101b512cc048f17534718dfdc6e5e95d.docx","sha256":"83bb524f000ddd1a74574726ffab582b101b512cc048f17534718dfdc6e5e95d","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"CROWNRIDGE HOUSING TRUST\nMajor Repairs Communications Roadmap\nExecutive decision version | Executive briefing and staged resident updates\nVersion\nIssue state\nIssue date\nChange in this version\nv0.1\nDecision draft\n16 Aug 2026\nInitial integrated roadmap: status model, decision gates, late-review route and date controls.\nExecutive decision\nDECISION FOR RAFAEL CASTELLANOS BY FRIDAY [DECISION DEADLINE - NOT A RELEASE DATE]  Record whether Zeynep Quintero may schedule a conditional executive briefing once Gate E evidence is complete; nominate the stakeholder approval and operational release authorities; and confirm how an incomplete contributor review will be handled if triggered. This decision does not authorise resident publication.\nObjective: give Rafael a safe, truthful scheduling decision for the executive briefing and staged resident updates. Current position: PLANNING / decision draft. A contributor approval may miss normal review, and draft dates are being treated as commitments. No state beyond Planning is asserted until its gate record exists. Proceeding incorrectly could send unvalidated information, an unworkable delivery route or an implied commitment to residents or delivery partners.\nDATE CONTROL  Draft, provisional and indicative dates are planning signals only - never confirmed resident release commitments. A calendar hold, circulated draft, attendance at a briefing or an invitation is not approval. Flintridge Freight must not produce or distribute resident packs without the written Gate O release confirmation.\nScope, audience and timing logic\nStream\nAudience / use\nMaterial / channel\nTiming status\nExecutive briefing\nRafael and senior decision-makers; supports an explicit schedule / hold decision.\nControlled briefing pack and meeting. It covers readiness, choices, open issues and evidence only; it does not substitute for resident release approval.\nAfter Gate E [CONDITIONAL]. A calendar entry becomes Scheduled only when Rafael's recorded decision permits Zeynep to issue it.\nStaged resident updates\nResidents; distinct external audience. Give only approved, plain-language material and access routes.\nApproved copy, printed packs via Flintridge Freight and confirmed accessible / alternative access arrangements. Include an approved route for questions, feedback or concerns.\nEach stage follows Gate O [CONDITIONAL]. It is Scheduled only after written release confirmation; it is Released only after distribution occurs.\nStatus model - the state must travel with every date\nPLANNED\nEDITORIALLY READY\nAPPROVED\nSCHEDULED\nRELEASED\nWork or indicative window being considered; no commitment.\nContent complete for a stated review; not stakeholder-approved.\nStakeholder decision recorded with conditions; not released.\nA Gate O-confirmed release window or recorded briefing booking.\nDistributed / delivered; follow-up and feedback active.\nAccountability and planning assumptions\nAccountability / handoffs\n- Producer-director: single accountable owner; maintains the roadmap, evidence and decision log; presents status to Rafael; owns the Flintridge interface.\n- Rafael Castellanos: Friday scheduling decision and final escalation for overdue approval or blocked dependency.\n- Zeynep Quintero: may schedule the executive briefing only against the recorded Gate E condition; labels any invitation conditional.\n- Sigrid Zalewski / Ivo Laurent: contributor reviewers; Paloma Bhandari is stakeholder liaison, not an implied approver.\nAssumptions requiring validation\n- A1 | Producer-director at Step 0: the available information can support a clearly bounded briefing and resident draft; unknowns remain visible.\n- A2 | Rafael by Friday [Decision]: a named stakeholder approval authority, operational release authority and resident-support lead will be recorded.\n- A3 | Producer-director + Flintridge before G3 [Conditional]: pack lead time, proof/handoff requirements and accessible-format needs can be confirmed.\n- A4 | Resident-support lead before G3 [Conditional]: an approved questions / feedback route and response capacity will be available.\n\n\nNORMAL PATH\nSequenced roadmap and decision gates\nTIMING BASIS  T0 = Friday's executive decision deadline, not a release date. Every future window below is labelled [PLANNING], [CONDITIONAL] or [SCHEDULED] as applicable. Critical path: information position -> contributor and operational review -> stakeholder approval -> controlled production / Flintridge handoff -> operational release confirmation -> resident stage and feedback.\nGate evidence is recorded before state changes. Any open material issue, missing approval or changed condition remains visible at the next gate; silence, circulation or attendance cannot close it.\nStage / state\nTiming / status\nAccountable owner\nEntry condition, decision output and next dependency\n0. Confirm brief and information position\nNow to T0 [PLANNING]\nProducer-director\nConfirm objective, audiences, available information, unknowns, resident access needs, decision roles and evidence sources before anything is treated as review-ready. Output: bounded communication brief and open-issue list.\n1A. Build / edit executive briefing pack\nNow to T0 [PLANNING]\nProducer-director\nUse only confirmed material from Step 0. Show choices, risks, assumptions and readiness evidence. Output: controlled draft for contributor review; no final briefing booking yet.\n1B. Develop staged resident messages\nNow to T0 [PLANNING]\nProducer-director + resident-support lead (to be named)\nPrepare plain-language, accessible resident-facing draft and a questions / feedback route. Separate it from internal approval material. Output: controlled draft, not a production file.\n2. Contributor / subject review\nBefore G1 / G2 cut-offs [CONDITIONAL]\nSigrid Zalewski and Ivo Laurent; Producer-director tracks\nReview the relevant draft before final approval; flag accuracy gaps and any content not validated. Output: recorded comments / confirmation. Missing review at cut-off invokes EX1, never an implied pass.\n3. Operational validation\nBefore G2 [CONDITIONAL]\nDesignated operational readiness lead + Producer-director\nCheck the proposed resident route can be delivered: support / feedback ownership, accessible alternatives, approved pack handoff requirements and Flintridge lead time. Output: evidence or a hold / revision.\nG1 / Gate E. Editorial readiness and briefing schedule decision\nAt T0 / Friday [DECISION, not release]\nRafael records decision; Zeynep acts only on record\nInput: controlled briefing pack, contributor status, open issues and exception record if any. PASS: editorial readiness is recorded and Rafael may let Zeynep schedule / invite conditionally. HOLD: revise, defer or escalate. This gate never approves resident release.\n4. Executive briefing delivery\nAfter G1 [CONDITIONAL -> SCHEDULED only on record]\nZeynep Quintero\nUse the controlled pack version and state its status in the invitation. End state: briefing held and its decisions / conditions recorded. It cannot convert resident material to Approved or Released.\nG2. Stakeholder approval\nAfter Steps 2-3 [CONDITIONAL]\nDesignated stakeholder approval authority; Paloma Bhandari is stakeholder liaison\nInput: editorially ready resident content, contributor record, operational validation, open issues and accessible-format plan. PASS: named authority records approval and conditions. FAIL: hold, revise / defer, or Rafael resolves blocked dependency.\n5. Production preparation and controlled handoff\nAfter G2 [CONDITIONAL]\nProducer-director -> Flintridge Freight\nPrepare only the recorded Approved version for chosen channels. Confirm proof, pack, lead-time and handoff needs with Flintridge. Handoff is marked 'no produce / no distribute' until Gate O written release confirmation.\nG3 / Gate O. Operational release confirmation\nBefore any resident release [CONDITIONAL]\nDesignated operational release authority (separate from editorial readiness)\nInput: G2 approval evidence, final controlled version, confirmed Flintridge readiness / lead time, accessible routes and resident questions owner. PASS: written release confirmation names scope and window; state becomes Scheduled. FAIL: hold and notify affected owners.\n6. Release each resident stage\nGate O-confirmed window [SCHEDULED only after written confirmation]\nOperational release authority; Flintridge executes printed-pack instruction\nDistribute only the released version and only for the authorised stage. End state: stage marked Released with evidence of distribution; any later stage returns to Gate O rather than inheriting approval.\n7. Post-release feedback and next-stage decision\nAfter each release [CONDITIONAL]\nResident-support lead + Producer-director\nMonitor questions, feedback, access issues and corrections; feed material changes into the controlled draft and re-run affected gates. End state: each stage is monitored, formally held, or closed in the decision log.\n\n\nCONTROLLED EXCEPTION AND CLOSE\nLate review, date misuse and decision record\nEX1 - incomplete contributor-review route\nControl point\nRequired action / owner\nStatus or schedule result\nTrigger / time-box\nA contributor approval is unavailable, or the stated review cut-off passes before G1 or G2 [CONDITIONAL]. Producer-director records it immediately and escalates to Rafael before the affected gate; if unresolved by that decision point, apply HOLD.\nNo automatic progression; timing remains conditional.\nAssess and contain\nProducer-director and an available qualified alternative reviewer assess whether the missing contribution affects accuracy, safety, resident understanding or release readiness. Remove, hold or clearly mark every unvalidated claim / section; do not silently carry it forward.\nAffected scope is held; only validated material may be considered.\nAlternative review and authorisation\nUse a delegated qualified reviewer where one is named; otherwise Rafael decides the escalation route. Proceeding under the exception needs explicit, attributable authorisation: Rafael for conditional briefing planning, plus the designated stakeholder / operational authority for any relevant downstream gate.\nException authorisation is a recorded decision, never inferred from silence, circulation or attendance.\nHard stop, schedule impact and notice\nIf required authority or safety-critical review is unresolved, resident release stays HOLD: no final vendor production or distribution. Zeynep may retain or withdraw only a conditional briefing hold. Producer-director notifies Rafael, Zeynep, contributors, Paloma, resident-support owner and Flintridge as scope / timing requires.\nBriefing may be conditionally planned; affected resident stage waits for normal controls.\nRecord and re-enter normal path\nRecord missing approval, reason, impact assessment, alternative review, compensating control, authorisation, conditions and changed timing. When available, complete the normal contributor review and repeat every affected G1 / G2 / G3 check before release.\nException closes only after normal review and affected decisions are complete.\nRisks, records and correction control\nRisk\nTrigger\nMitigation / accountable owner\nMissing review\nReview cut-off missed / contributor unavailable.\nEX1, content containment and Rafael escalation; owner: Producer-director.\nProvisional date treated as commitment\nDraft calendar, pack or vendor query is presented as a release promise.\nProducer-director corrects the affected record promptly, tells Rafael, Zeynep and Flintridge / recipients as relevant, marks the old date withdrawn / Planning, resets the controlled schedule, and logs reason and impact.\nDelivery or support not ready\nFlintridge lead time, accessible route or questions capacity is not confirmed.\nOperational release authority holds G3; Producer-director communicates the changed status truthfully and seeks a revised conditional window.\nInconsistent or unsupported audience messaging\nDifferent copies, unvalidated claim, or unclear resident access / feedback route.\nHold affected copy; align a single controlled version and re-run the relevant review / approval gate. Owner: Producer-director.\nControl record\nSOURCE OF TRUTH  The Producer-director maintains this controlled roadmap and decision log. The log keeps planning assumptions, open issues and recorded authorisations distinct. For every gate, retain the decision-maker, date / timing, rationale, conditions and evidence of contributor approval, stakeholder approval or release authorisation. For any message, owner, timing or status change, record the reason and impact, then update the executive summary, roadmap and release-facing / Flintridge instruction together so no conflicting copy survives.\nCurrent decision position\nCURRENT STATE: PLANNING / DECISION DRAFT  No approval or release outcome is claimed. By Friday [DECISION DEADLINE - NOT A RELEASE DATE], Rafael should record whether Zeynep may arrange only a conditional executive briefing once Gate E evidence exists, identify the approval / release authorities, and decide the exception route if it is triggered. The next concrete decision is G2 stakeholder approval [CONDITIONAL] once Steps 2-3 evidence exists - not a resident release date. Until then, resident updates and any Flintridge activity remain PLANNED or HOLD, never a confirmed release.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted artifact is a readable DOCX with the requested filename and extension.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"The submitted file name and successful DOCX opening.","grader_hint":"Inspect the submission directory and open the file with a standard office library or renderer. Award only for the exact requested filename and a readable DOCX; this is a scaffolding item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The roadmap immediately makes clear that it concerns Crownridge Housing Trust's major repairs communications and is intended to support Rafael Castellanos's scheduling decision.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Title, opening summary, or decision statement.","grader_hint":"Read the first third of the document. The purpose and decision should be apparent without searching through the roadmap."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered document does not exceed three pages.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render the DOCX with a normal office renderer at its default page setup and count pages. Do not award if content is hidden in text boxes or clipped."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies its current version or issue state and records what changed in that version.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Version, issue date, and change note or equivalent.","grader_hint":"Look for a compact document-control statement. A version plus a meaningful change summary is required; a bare date is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The roadmap states the immediate executive decision required rather than merely describing communications activity.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive summary or decision box.","grader_hint":"The reader should know what Rafael must decide about briefing and resident-update scheduling, and what the decision depends on."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The scope covers both the executive briefing and the staged resident updates, without silently substituting one for the other.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Scope statement and roadmap rows or sections.","grader_hint":"Confirm that both communication streams are named and represented in the plan."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap explains the meaning of its status categories or equivalent markers well enough to distinguish work-in-progress, approved material, scheduled release, and released material.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Legend, status notes, or definitions embedded in the roadmap.","grader_hint":"Accept different terminology if the four operational states remain unambiguous. Do not require particular invented codes."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The opening summary is concise but tells an executive the objective, current uncertainty, immediate decision, and consequence of proceeding incorrectly.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Executive summary.","grader_hint":"Assess whether a senior reader can understand the situation and stakes in under a minute, without reading every schedule row."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies the accountable owner for maintaining the roadmap and for presenting its status to the decision maker.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Owner field or governance statement.","grader_hint":"A named person or clearly defined role must be accountable; a generic team label alone is weak evidence."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap states the planning horizon or decision deadline relative to Friday and distinguishes that deadline from any release date.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing statement.","grader_hint":"Check that Friday is treated as the decision deliverable deadline, not automatically as a publication commitment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies the executive or senior decision-maker audience and states what that audience needs from the roadmap.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience or purpose statement.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit senior-reader use case such as approving, holding, or conditionally scheduling communications."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap identifies residents as a distinct audience and does not treat resident communications as interchangeable with the executive briefing.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience and communication-stream descriptions.","grader_hint":"The resident audience should have separate message, channel, or readiness considerations."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap distinguishes internal working stakeholders from external or resident recipients.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audience map or stakeholder fields.","grader_hint":"Award when the document shows who reviews or approves material versus who receives it."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document uses language a senior decision maker can scan and understand without communications-production jargon being left undefined.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered prose and labels.","grader_hint":"Judge clarity, not stylistic preference. Acronyms or specialist terms should be explained or avoided."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap identifies how audience, message, channel, and timing relate for the executive briefing and resident updates.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Communication-stream entries.","grader_hint":"Each stream should connect its intended recipients with an appropriate communication form and timing logic."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document separates internal approval communications from external resident-facing release communications.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Schedule, audience map, or release controls.","grader_hint":"Confirm that an internal briefing or approval event is not presented as the resident release itself."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The resident-facing plan accounts for plain language and accessibility or alternative access needs.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Resident communication requirements.","grader_hint":"Accept accessible formats, language/access needs, readable wording, or equivalent professional controls."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap identifies a route for questions, feedback, or concerns arising from the resident updates.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Resident communications row or post-release control.","grader_hint":"A usable contact or feedback owner/channel must be stated without inventing unsupported contact details."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sequence starts with confirming the communication objective, audience, and available information before material is treated as ready for review.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Earliest roadmap milestone.","grader_hint":"The opening activity should establish the brief and information position, not begin with publication."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan includes creation or editing of the executive briefing content before executive briefing scheduling is treated as final.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Briefing workstream.","grader_hint":"There must be a content-readiness stage distinct from the meeting date."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan includes development of resident-facing messages before resident production or distribution.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Resident-update workstream.","grader_hint":"Look for drafting, editing, or message preparation as a distinct stage."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sequence provides a contributor or subject-matter review stage before final approval.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Review milestone and dependency.","grader_hint":"Contributor review must be placed before final stakeholder approval, unless an explicit exception branch handles the deviation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sequence includes operational validation of what can actually be delivered to residents before release.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Operational-readiness milestone.","grader_hint":"Accept checks of service timing, delivery capability, resident support, or equivalent operational feasibility."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sequence includes stakeholder approval as a distinct state after content and operational checks.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Approval gate.","grader_hint":"A draft being editorially complete must not itself count as stakeholder approval."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan includes production preparation for the chosen channels before release.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Production milestone.","grader_hint":"Look for preparation of briefing materials, resident copy, printed packs, or other selected outputs."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan includes an operational release or distribution stage that is distinct from production preparation.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Release milestone.","grader_hint":"Production complete and release actually occurring should be distinguishable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap includes a post-release monitoring or feedback stage for the staged resident updates.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Post-release milestone.","grader_hint":"Award for planned monitoring, response handling, or lessons capture after distribution."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The order of milestones reflects dependencies rather than listing activities as an unordered catalogue.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Roadmap sequence and dependency indicators.","grader_hint":"Trace at least the path from content creation through review, approval, preparation, and release."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each major transition states what must be true before the next stage can begin.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entry criteria, gate conditions, or dependency notes.","grader_hint":"The roadmap should contain operational readiness criteria, not only dates and activity names."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The sequence has a defined end state for both the executive briefing and the resident-update stream.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final milestones or outcome statement.","grader_hint":"The reader should know when each stream is considered complete or handed into monitoring."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap names one accountable owner for overall coordination rather than leaving accountability shared ambiguously.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Overall owner field.","grader_hint":"A single accountable owner or role must be visible."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every major milestone has an identifiable owner responsible for completing or advancing it.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Owner column or equivalent milestone annotations.","grader_hint":"Sample all major rows, including review, approval, production, release, and monitoring; do not award for a single generic team owner."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The person or role providing contributor approval is distinguished from the person editing or coordinating the material.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Responsibility assignments.","grader_hint":"The roadmap must show that drafting and approval are not silently conflated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The stakeholder approval authority is identified separately from the contributor reviewer.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Approval gate owner.","grader_hint":"Accept a role title or named person, provided authority is clear."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The person or role authorising operational release is identified separately from editorial readiness.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Release gate and owner.","grader_hint":"Look for explicit release authority or equivalent control; editorial completion alone is not enough."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap gives Zeynep Quintero a clear responsibility or decision condition for scheduling the executive briefing.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Briefing schedule row or owner field.","grader_hint":"The document should make clear when Zeynep may schedule and what status she must rely on."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The interface with Flintridge Freight is assigned to an owner and includes a readiness or handoff condition for printed resident packs.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Vendor dependency and owner.","grader_hint":"The roadmap should not treat a print vendor as an invisible automatic step."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan identifies a deputy, fallback owner, or escalation arrangement for an unavailable contributor or coordinator.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Backup responsibility or exception branch.","grader_hint":"A named alternative role or clear escalation route is sufficient; do not require an invented person."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap identifies who resolves an overdue approval or blocked dependency.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Escalation owner.","grader_hint":"The escalation path must end with an accountable decision-maker, not merely say 'escalate'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Responsibilities and handoffs are concrete enough that the working team can act without asking who owns the next state.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole roadmap.","grader_hint":"Judge whether ownership remains clear at transitions and under the exception path, not just in a separate stakeholder list."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each major gate states the decision or authorisation it is intended to produce.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Gate descriptions.","grader_hint":"A gate should say what is being accepted, held, revised, or released."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each major gate identifies the decision-maker or approving role.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Gate owner fields.","grader_hint":"Check editorial, stakeholder, operational, and release gates."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The decision inputs for each major gate are stated, including the relevant content, review status, and operational readiness evidence.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Gate criteria or decision notes.","grader_hint":"Award when the document tells the approver what evidence to review rather than only naming the approver."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap gives each gate a decision point or cutoff that is distinguishable from the eventual public release date.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Schedule or gate timing.","grader_hint":"Decision timing may be relative, but it must not be confused with confirmed publication timing."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan gives explicit pass conditions for moving from editorial readiness to approval and from approval to release.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Gate entry and exit criteria.","grader_hint":"Look for completeness, approval, operational feasibility, and release-readiness conditions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R46","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan states what happens when a gate is not passed, including hold, revise, defer, or escalation treatment.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Gate failure branch.","grader_hint":"A gate with only a pass path is incomplete."},{"rubric_item_id":"R47","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap requires unresolved material issues or missing approvals to remain visible at the decision point.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Open issues or gate checklist.","grader_hint":"Award when the document prevents unresolved work from disappearing into a generic 'approved' state."},{"rubric_item_id":"R48","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan provides for recording each gate decision, its date or timing, and its rationale or conditions.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision-log or governance instruction.","grader_hint":"A record requirement is sufficient; the artifact need not contain future decisions as if they had already happened."},{"rubric_item_id":"R49","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document distinguishes the decision to schedule the executive briefing from the decision to release resident communications.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision map or separate gates.","grader_hint":"A briefing can be scheduled or held under different conditions from resident publication; the roadmap must show that distinction."},{"rubric_item_id":"R50","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap states that silence, attendance, or circulation of a draft does not by itself constitute approval.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Approval-control language.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent language that prevents implied approval."},{"rubric_item_id":"R51","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document describes the normal editorial-review path sufficiently to show where the missing contributor approval belongs.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Normal-path sequence.","grader_hint":"The exception cannot be evaluated unless the normal review point and its downstream effect are visible."},{"rubric_item_id":"R52","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap defines a trigger for invoking the incomplete-review path, such as a review deadline passing or an approval being unavailable.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception trigger.","grader_hint":"Look for a concrete trigger rather than an open-ended statement that exceptions may occur."},{"rubric_item_id":"R53","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The exception path requires assessing whether the missing contribution affects accuracy, safety, resident understanding, or release readiness.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception assessment criteria.","grader_hint":"The document should not treat every missing review as either harmless or automatically fatal without assessment."},{"rubric_item_id":"R54","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The exception path identifies an alternative qualified reviewer, delegated authority, or escalation route rather than simply bypassing the missing contributor.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception branch owner or authority.","grader_hint":"Award only when the alternate route retains accountable review or approval."},{"rubric_item_id":"R55","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document requires explicit authorisation for proceeding under the exception path.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception approval condition.","grader_hint":"The wording must require a conscious, attributable decision; automatic progression is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R56","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap requires the missing approval, the reason for its absence, and the compensating review or control to be recorded.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception record requirement.","grader_hint":"Look for an auditable exception record, not merely an email or verbal workaround."},{"rubric_item_id":"R57","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The exception path allows unvalidated claims or sections to be removed, held, or clearly marked rather than silently carrying them into release.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Treatment of affected content.","grader_hint":"The roadmap must show how content-level risk is contained."},{"rubric_item_id":"R58","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan states how the schedule changes when the exception path is used, including what remains possible and what must wait.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception schedule branch.","grader_hint":"A generic 'replan' is insufficient; the branch must identify affected communications or gates."},{"rubric_item_id":"R59","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The exception path includes a time-box or escalation deadline for resolving the missing review.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception timing.","grader_hint":"Accept a relative cutoff tied to a gate or release decision."},{"rubric_item_id":"R60","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document prevents resident release when a required authority or safety-critical review remains unresolved.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Release hold condition.","grader_hint":"The exception route may permit controlled progress, but it must retain a hard stop where required authority is absent."},{"rubric_item_id":"R61","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan states how the normal review is resumed or completed after the immediate exception has been managed.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Re-entry or follow-up review instruction.","grader_hint":"Look for a route back to the normal control, not a permanent bypass."},{"rubric_item_id":"R62","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The exception path includes notification or handoff to people affected by the changed timing or scope.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Exception communications step.","grader_hint":"This may include Rafael, Zeynep, contributors, the print vendor, or resident-facing owners as appropriate."},{"rubric_item_id":"R63","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap differentiates at least planned, editorially ready, approved, scheduled, and released states, or clearly equivalent states.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Status model.","grader_hint":"Do not award if 'ready' or 'published' is used ambiguously for multiple states."},{"rubric_item_id":"R64","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Every future date or timing window is visibly labelled according to whether it is indicative, conditional, approved, scheduled, or completed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Date labels in summary and schedule.","grader_hint":"Inspect all prominent dates, not only the legend. Status must travel with the date."},{"rubric_item_id":"R65","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document contains a prominent warning or control that draft or provisional dates must not be treated as confirmed release commitments.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Visible warning, callout, or equivalent control.","grader_hint":"Search for terms such as provisional, indicative, draft, confirmed, approved, or equivalent. The warning must actively instruct readers what not to do."},{"rubric_item_id":"R66","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap prevents a calendar entry, draft circulation, or briefing invitation from being interpreted as release approval.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Status logic and scheduling controls.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit distinction between holding time and authorising publication."},{"rubric_item_id":"R67","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan requires final release confirmation after the relevant approval and operational readiness gates.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Release control.","grader_hint":"A date may be planned earlier, but release must require a later affirmative confirmation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R68","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies where the authoritative current status is maintained or how the team should determine which status is current.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Status-control instruction.","grader_hint":"Accept a named controlled roadmap, decision log, or equivalent without requiring an invented system name."},{"rubric_item_id":"R69","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap requires status and date changes to be updated consistently across the executive summary, schedule, and release-facing instructions.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Change-control statement.","grader_hint":"Award for a control against conflicting copies or sections."},{"rubric_item_id":"R70","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan provides a correction or retraction route if a provisional date has already been presented as a commitment.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Misuse-response or correction control.","grader_hint":"The roadmap should say who corrects the record, who is notified, and how the affected schedule is reset."},{"rubric_item_id":"R71","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan prevents Flintridge Freight from producing or distributing resident packs solely because a draft date appears on a working schedule.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Vendor handoff or release condition.","grader_hint":"Look for an affirmative release instruction or approval evidence required before print/distribution."},{"rubric_item_id":"R72","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The warning against provisional-date misuse is prominent enough that a busy reader or delivery partner is unlikely to miss it.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document hierarchy and wording.","grader_hint":"Judge placement, visual emphasis, and plainness. A buried sentence in a dense note should not receive full credit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R73","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The schedule shows dependencies between approvals, production, vendor handoff, briefing scheduling, and resident release.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Dependency markers or linked milestone descriptions.","grader_hint":"Trace the critical dependencies across both communication streams."},{"rubric_item_id":"R74","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The schedule uses relative timing, dates, or windows consistently enough to show sequencing and does not mix incompatible timing conventions.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timeline.","grader_hint":"Exact calendar dates are not required. Award for a coherent, interpretable time basis."},{"rubric_item_id":"R75","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Major milestones include an indication of duration, deadline, or time needed to complete the work.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Timing fields.","grader_hint":"A schedule consisting only of milestone names is not sufficiently actionable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R76","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap identifies the critical path or the dependencies most likely to control the earliest possible release.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Critical-path note or schedule logic.","grader_hint":"Look for the approval, operational, or production dependency that determines release timing."},{"rubric_item_id":"R77","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document includes a contingency for delay that preserves truthful status communication rather than simply moving the date silently.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Delay branch and communication control.","grader_hint":"The contingency should address both schedule impact and how the changed status is communicated."},{"rubric_item_id":"R78","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap shows how the executive briefing and staged resident updates can be sequenced when they cannot all proceed at the same time.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Two-stream schedule or conditional branch.","grader_hint":"Award when the document explains the relationship between the streams under normal and delayed conditions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R79","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The timing accounts for the lead time or handoff needed by the printed-pack supplier before resident distribution.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Flintridge Freight dependency.","grader_hint":"The plan need not invent a number, but it must recognise that printing/distribution requires a controlled handoff and time."},{"rubric_item_id":"R80","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap identifies the next concrete decision point after Friday or the current planning deadline.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Forward decision timing.","grader_hint":"Accept a relative decision window, provided it is distinct from an unsupported release promise."},{"rubric_item_id":"R81","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document lists assumptions separately from confirmed facts or decisions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assumptions section or labelled fields.","grader_hint":"Assumptions should be visibly labelled and not embedded as unconditional claims."},{"rubric_item_id":"R82","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each material assumption has an owner or validation point.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Assumption owner or validation column.","grader_hint":"A list of unowned assumptions is incomplete."},{"rubric_item_id":"R83","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap identifies risks arising from missing review, premature commitment, operational inability to deliver, or inconsistent audience messaging.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk or issue register.","grader_hint":"The risks should be connected to this communications situation rather than generic project risks."},{"rubric_item_id":"R84","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each material risk has a mitigation, owner, or trigger for escalation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Risk controls.","grader_hint":"Award when the roadmap turns risks into executable controls."},{"rubric_item_id":"R85","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The roadmap requires evidence of approval or release authorisation to be retained.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Governance or recordkeeping statement.","grader_hint":"The document should specify the type of evidence or record, without inventing an unavailable repository."},{"rubric_item_id":"R86","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document includes a simple change-control rule for revisions to messages, owners, timing, or release status.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Change-control instruction.","grader_hint":"Look for a requirement to record the change, reason, owner, and impact or an equivalent set of controls."},{"rubric_item_id":"R87","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The plan provides for recording decisions and open issues so that later readers can distinguish an authorised change from an assumption.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Decision/open-issue control.","grader_hint":"Award for an auditable distinction between decisions, unresolved items, and planning assumptions."},{"rubric_item_id":"R88","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document avoids unsupported programme specifics while still giving enough professional structure to execute the communications work.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document.","grader_hint":"Check for invented repair dates, resident impacts, approval outcomes, quantities, legal claims, or commitments. Do not penalise reasonable planning assumptions when they are labelled as such."},{"rubric_item_id":"R89","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The rendered roadmap has clear hierarchy, consistent labels, readable density, and visual flow suitable for quick executive scanning.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered DOCX.","grader_hint":"Assess usability rather than decoration. Tables or timelines must remain legible, and headings must make the sequence and decision controls easy to find."},{"rubric_item_id":"R90","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document closes with the current decision position or approval state and does not end with an unowned list of future actions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Final section or closing statement.","grader_hint":"The ending should tell Rafael what is currently authorised, provisional, blocked, or awaiting decision. It may identify accountable actions, but should not dissolve into a generic next-steps list."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a small housing-trust communications programme in which a producer-director must turn an uncertain editorial and approval position into an executable roadmap for senior review. The document must support both executive briefing preparation and resident-facing release coordination.","difficulty_sources":["Professional knowledge of executive and resident communications sequencing","Distinguishing editorial readiness, stakeholder approval, scheduling, and operational release","Designing a controlled exception route for missing contributor approval","Actively preventing provisional dates from becoming misleading commitments","Producing an actionable, scan-friendly roadmap without inventing programme facts"],"authoring_plan":{"purpose":"Construct a compact but complete executive communications roadmap that demonstrates professional normative practice without relying on supplied reference files.","gold_document_constraints":["Create the requested DOCX and keep the rendered document within three pages.","Use Crownridge Housing Trust, Rafael Castellanos, Zeynep Quintero, Sigrid Zalewski, Ivo Laurent, Paloma Bhandari, and Flintridge Freight only where useful and consistently.","Do not invent facts about the major repairs programme, resident impacts, approval outcomes, contact details, dates, volumes, or service commitments.","Use relative timing or clearly labelled planning windows where a calendar date is not known.","Include a concise executive purpose and decision summary, a visible status model, an ordered roadmap for the briefing and resident updates, owners, gate criteria, assumptions, risks, dependencies, and change/decision controls.","Make the normal review route visible and create a separate controlled exception route for a contributor approval that cannot arrive on time.","The exception route must include a trigger, materiality or content-impact assessment, qualified alternative review or escalation, explicit authorisation, documentation of the missing approval and compensating control, treatment of unvalidated content, schedule impact, a hard stop where required authority remains absent, and a route back to normal review.","Actively prevent the misuse of draft dates by labelling date status throughout, distinguishing calendar holds from approval and release, controlling the Flintridge Freight handoff, requiring affirmative release confirmation, and providing a correction route if a provisional date has been treated as committed.","Assign overall accountability and named or role-based ownership to every significant milestone and gate, including briefing scheduling, contributor review, stakeholder approval, operational release, resident support, vendor handoff, and escalation.","End with the current decision or approval position, not an unowned action list."],"gold_derivation":{"sequence":"Derive the roadmap from professional communications production practice: establish the brief and information position, develop each audience-specific communication, conduct contributor and operational review, obtain stakeholder approval, prepare channels and printed packs, confirm release, and monitor resident response.","status_logic":"Represent planning, editorial readiness, approval, scheduling, and release as distinct states. Ensure a draft or indicative date cannot advance state merely because it appears on a calendar or is circulated.","exception_logic":"Show a normal path and a branch for late or missing contributor approval. The branch should preserve accountability and truthfulness rather than bypassing review. It should permit only controlled progression, with unvalidated claims held or removed and resident release blocked when required authority is absent.","ownership_logic":"Use the supplied people and vendor as realistic owners or interfaces: Rafael for the executive decision, Zeynep for briefing scheduling, named contributors for review or operational input, and Flintridge Freight for the controlled printed-pack handoff. Avoid assigning unsupported organisational powers; role descriptions may be used where authority is not established.","schedule_logic":"Use coherent relative timing tied to the Friday decision horizon. Include dependencies, gate cutoffs, vendor lead time, contingency treatment, and separate timing logic for the executive briefing and staged resident updates.","governance_logic":"Include labelled assumptions, owners for validating them, risks tied to late review and premature commitments, decision and approval records, version/change information, and consistent status updates across the summary and detailed roadmap.","quality_logic":"Render and inspect the final DOCX for page count, legibility, hierarchy, scanability, and absence of clipped or hidden content. Confirm that all 90 rubric predicates are represented by distinct evidence and that no criterion depends on an invented exact code or unsupported factual answer."},"offline_feasibility_check":"The artifact can be authored and rendered offline with python-docx and LibreOffice or another standard office renderer. All substantive checks rely on the supplied request and occupational communications-production knowledge; no live information or external source is needed."},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"executive_communications_roadmap","filename":"crownridge_executive_communications_roadmap.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"An executive-ready project roadmap for the executive briefing and staged resident communications concerning the major repairs programme.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be no more than three pages when rendered.","Must distinguish editorial readiness, stakeholder approval, operational release, and post-release follow-up.","Must identify owners, decision points, assumptions, dependencies, exception handling, and status of dates.","Must not present provisional dates as confirmed releases or invent unsupported programme facts.","Must be usable by a senior decision maker and the working communications team without oral explanation."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"project_plan","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"producers_directors","sector":"information","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":3.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"domain_expert","reviewer_policy":"A communications production reviewer should inspect the rendered document against every rubric item, then perform a second pass specifically for provisional-date misuse, incomplete-review handling, unsupported claims, and page count.","minimum_reviewers":1,"known_limitations":["No programme source material is supplied, so factual accuracy can only be assessed as avoidance of unsupported claims and correct labelling of assumptions.","Relative timing is acceptable because the request provides Friday but no calendar date or fixed programme milestones."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:12:59+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:12:59+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:12:59+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T16:12:59+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/7816828f15ef5632","declared_deliverables":["crownridge_executive_communications_roadmap.docx"]};
